            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Cuts a signal into overlapping windows - every run of window neighbouring values, one after another, each starting one step on - and stacks them as the rows of an array.\n\nThat turns any node that measures a whole array into a rolling one. np.mean of the windows is a rolling mean; np.ptp, the range within each moment; np.std, how jittery each stretch is; np.fft.rfft, a spectrogram. A whole recording is analysed window by window in one step, with every result lined up in a row.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.sliding_window_view <window: int> <step: int>\nDefaults: window 4, step 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.sliding_window_view 30 10\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal (a list or array). Every input is cut and sent.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each window. It cannot be longer than the signal.\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window starts after the last: 1 (the default) gives every window, 10 every tenth - fewer windows, overlapping less.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way the signal runs: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, axis 0 windows the frames, keeping the joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nwindows:\nOne row per window. For a list of n values and window w there are n - w + 1 windows (fewer with a larger step); for more axes, the window becomes a new last axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 6 in windows of 3: 1 2 3, 2 3 4, 3 4 5 and 4 5 6. np.mean along axis 1 - across each row - gives a rolling mean: 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.rolling_buffer keeps the latest values of a stream. np.convolve with equal weights is a rolling mean in one step. np.split cuts into pieces that do not overlap.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Cuts a signal into overlapping windows - every run of window neighbouring values, one after another, each starting one step on - and stacks them as the rows of an array.\n\nThat turns any node that measures a whole array into a rolling one. np.mean of the windows is a rolling mean; np.ptp, the range within each moment; np.std, how jittery each stretch is; np.fft.rfft, a spectrogram. A whole recording is analysed window by window in one step, with every result lined up in a row.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.sliding_window_view <window: int> <step: int>\nDefaults: window 4, step 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.sliding_window_view 30 10\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal (a list or array). Every input is cut and sent.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each window. It cannot be longer than the signal.\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window starts after the last: 1 (the default) gives every window, 10 every tenth - fewer windows, overlapping less.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way the signal runs: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, axis 0 windows the frames, keeping the joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nwindows:\nOne row per window. For a list of n values and window w there are n - w + 1 windows (fewer with a larger step); for more axes, the window becomes a new last axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 6 in windows of 3: 1 2 3, 2 3 4, 3 4 5 and 4 5 6. np.mean along axis 1 - across each row - gives a rolling mean: 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.rolling_buffer keeps the latest values of a stream. np.convolve with equal weights is a rolling mean in one step. np.split cuts into pieces that do not overlap."
        },
